                <pb facs="0771" n="659" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg659"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.61/58: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-09-03T17:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 3,
                                1919, 5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received September 3, 2:04
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>2959. Following from [Cole at] Archangel.</p>
                <p>1402, September 2, 7 p.m. Embassy and consulate staffs sailing for London on the
                    steamer <hi rendition="#italic">Kildonan Castle</hi> today except Albert, Flack
                    and myself who expect to leave on or about September 7. See no reason for United
                    States Steamship <hi rendition="#italic">Des Moines</hi> remaining after my
                    departure.</p>
                <p>Japanese and Serbian Chargé d’Affaires leaving today. Chinese Chargé d’Affaires
                    already gone. French Chargé d’Affaires will remain somewhat longer to demobilize
                    Russian-French legion. On September 1st British Chargé d’Affaires ceased
                    functioning as such either here or with Foreign Office, becoming “Diplomatic
                    Adviser” to Commander-in-Chief Rawlinson. Movement of British troops has begun
                    and expected to be completed as planned by September 30th. Cole.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
